# Target C-II — `t<=1.844` forces retained dominant mass `>2.217D` and at most `541` switching diameter edges

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** scope-safe matched atomic gap `g=0.007707`; residual stability/shared retention/complement incidence; existing `z>2.2069` phase theorem for the scope gate; optimized adjacent-backbone and switching-caterpillar theorems  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_t1844_retention_2217.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The previous uniform `t<=1.844` phase theorem proved retained dominant outer-occurrence mass

\[
z>2.2069-o(1).
\]

The residual-coupled incidence inequality is strong enough at the same endpoint to
bootstrap this to

\[
\boxed{z>2.217-o(1).}
\tag{R217.1}

This simultaneously shrinks the complete Woodall/rigidity defect and the full actual
diameter-edge density.  On the optimized adjacent backbone, the finite switching cusp
therefore drops from at most `542` to at most

\[
\boxed{541}
\]

actual diameter edges for all sufficiently large survivors.

---

## 1. Residual-stability scope remains valid

Use

\[
C_{707}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.007707,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

and residual variables

\[
2\delta+x+h
=R_t:={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_{707},
\qquad
x={\kappa\over2},
\qquad
\kappa={d_H+s_Z\over D}.
\tag{R217.2}

The already-proved `z>2.2069` input gives

\[
\delta>F(2.2069)-o(1),
\qquad
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6).
\]

Hence at `t<=1.844`, even before the new bootstrap,

\[
U_{net}
\le {9t\over2}-2\pi-2F(2.2069)+o(1)
<1.916+o(1),
\]

well inside the hostile-audited residual-stability range `U_net<=2.013`.

---

## 2. Residual slack is below `0.00225`

Put

\[
T=1.844,
\qquad
H=0.00225.
\]

At the adverse point `t=T`, `h=H`, `x=0`, write

\[
\delta_H={R_T-H\over2}
\]

and

\[
A_H
=
\pi-{T\over2}-1.05H
-{1.05^2H^2\over2(1-\delta_H)}.
\]

The exact outward certificate proves

\[
\boxed{F(A_H)-\delta_H>3.9\times10^{-6}.}
\tag{R217.3}
\]

The parent survivor function is still strictly decreasing in both `x` and `h` on this
box (`Q_x<-2.7`, `Q_h<-2.5`) and increasing in `t`.  Consequently every survivor with
`t<=T` has

\[
\boxed{0\le h<0.00225-o(1).}
\tag{R217.4}

---

## 3. Capped retention at `Z=2.217` is impossible

Assume

\[
z\le Z:=2.217.
\]

The hostile-audited shared-retention/complement-incidence package gives

\[
z+
{1.05^2h^2+\delta-F(z)\over2(1-\delta)}
\ge
\pi-{t\over2}-x-1.05h-o(1)
\tag{R217.5}
\]

on the energy-feasible side `F(z)<=delta`.

As in the audited `1.8436` retention proof, the left side is increasing in `z`, and on
`delta>=F(Z)` the capped survivor excess increases with `x` until the boundary
`delta=F(Z)` is reached.  Put

\[
h_*=R_T-2F(Z).
\]

The exact enclosure gives

\[
\boxed{h_*=0.00227275640835\ldots>H.}
\tag{R217.6}

Thus for every allowed `h<=H` the energy boundary is reached at the nonnegative value

\[
x=h_*-h.
\]

On that boundary the capped excess increases with `h`, so the adverse point is `h=H`.
The exact certificate gives

\[
\boxed{
Z+{1.05^2H^2\over2(1-F(Z))}
-\pi+{T\over2}+h_*+0.05H
<-2.0\times10^{-4}.
}
\tag{R217.7}

The complementary `delta<F(Z)` branch reduces to the same boundary using the parent
`Q_x<0` monotonicity, exactly as in the hostile-audited predecessor.  Therefore the
assumption `z<=Z` is impossible and (R217.1) follows.

The certificate also records

\[
\boxed{F(2.217)>0.0501046613420736.}
\tag{R217.8}

---

## 4. The complete topology defect is below `0.004546D`

Since `h>=0`, equations (R217.2) and (R217.8) imply at `t<=T`

\[
{\kappa\over2}
\le
R_T-2F(2.217)+o(1).
\]

The exact certificate obtains

\[
\boxed{\kappa<0.004545513+o(1).}
\tag{R217.9}

Let `h_H` denote the number of actual diameter edges.  The exact ledger

\[
q-k=h_H-s_Z=tD,
\qquad s_Z\le k
\]

gives

\[
{h_H\over D}
=t+{s_Z\over D}
\le t+\kappa.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{{h_H\over D}<1.848545513+o(1).}
\tag{R217.10}

and the total adjacent interval-switch budget on any selected backbone is

\[
\boxed{
\sum_iN_i
\le2h_H
<3.697091026D+o(D).
}
\tag{R217.11}

---

## 5. Optimized path plus Pareto selection gives at most `541` rows

The optimized backbone theorem supplies path density

\[
p=0.00682246453482\ldots .
\]

Hence the count-only average satisfies

\[
{3.697091026\over p}
=541.899633\ldots<542.
\tag{R217.12}

To keep the phase and switching-multiplier properties on the same boundary, apply the
Pareto union bound with

\[
n=542,
\qquad a=b=10^{14}.
\]

The exact certificate checks that the count-bad, multiplier-bad, and phase-bad density
sum remains below `p` by more than `10^{-6}`.  Thus one internal boundary satisfies

\[
N_i<542,
\]

\[
B_i=O(D^{-1}),
\]

and

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})=O(D^{-1}).
\]

Since `N_i` is integer,

\[
\boxed{N_i\le541.}
\tag{R217.13}

The switching rows are exactly one layer cut of the straight-line diameter thrackle,
so the switching-caterpillar theorem upgrades this to

\[
\boxed{
H_{sw}\text{ is a caterpillar forest with at most }541\text{ edges}.
}
\tag{R217.14}

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The live `t<=1.844` full-core obstruction now has the source-level normal form

```text
retained dominant outer mass > 2.217D
complete Woodall/rigidity defect k < 0.004546D
actual diameter-edge density < 1.848546D
one optimized adjacent O-O layer interface
+ at most 541 switching diameter edges
+ switching graph is a caterpillar forest
+ switching beta mass O(1/D)
+ leaf phase mismatch O(1/D)
+ exact opposite finite two-cut shear
```

The scalar `t` endpoint is not closed by this theorem.  The remaining obstruction is
still the full-support max-function cusp on a finite active diameter interface.  The
new point is that both the global topology defect and the finite interface have become
quantitatively very small at the same uniform `1.844` endpoint.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only residual/retention and finite-cusp strengthening.
C-II and Level I remain open.
